WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.660
Why does a growth factor for nerve cells have a receptor named after a tumour?

2
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.050
Start with the first neurotrophin receptor anyone cloned.

3
00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.170
It could not explain survival.

4
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:20.770
In 1986, Moses Chao and colleagues transferred human DNA into mouse cells.

5
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:25.310
They found cells that made the human receptor, and cloned its gene.

6
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.140
Eric Shooter's laboratory reported the rat receptor the next year.

7
00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:35.243
This protein, p75, bound NGF with low affinity.

8
00:00:36.183 --> 00:00:37.183
It had no kinase.

9
00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:41.283
Neurons were known to have higher affinity sites as well.

10
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.213
On its own, p75 could not account for them.

11
00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:50.167
The kinase came from somewhere else entirely: a human colon tumour.

12
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.820
In 1986, Mariano Barbacid's laboratory described an oncogene from that tumour.

13
00:00:57.883 --> 00:01:02.243
Part of a tropomyosin gene had fused to the gene for a tyrosine kinase.

14
00:01:03.217 --> 00:01:06.277
The fusion kept the kinase on, without any ligand.

15
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.100
The name comes from that fusion: tropomyosin receptor kinase.

16
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:16.313
The normal gene encoded a membrane receptor with no known ligand.

17
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:20.627
If DCC from the netrin lesson comes to mind, it should.

18
00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:26.630
Here again, a gene first studied in cancer had a job in wiring the nervous system.

19
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.320
Now a test. Take a fibroblast that makes no Trk receptor and ignores NGF.

20
00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:36.873
Give it the gene for the normal Trk protein.

21
00:01:37.813 --> 00:01:40.373
Then add NGF. What should happen?

22
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:53.107
In spring 1991, two groups showed that NGF binds the Trk protein and causes its

23
00:01:53.107 --> 00:01:54.547
tyrosine phosphorylation.

24
00:01:55.550 --> 00:02:00.710
Barbacid's group also found that fibroblasts carrying Trk divided in response to

25
00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:03.490
NGF, without needing p75.

26
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:08.453
The orphan kinase from a tumour was a receptor for NGF.

27
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:14.497
Earlier, in Barbacid's laboratory, Ruediger Klein went looking for the Trk gene in

28
00:02:14.497 --> 00:02:15.497
mouse brain.

29
00:02:16.017 --> 00:02:19.557
In his later account, he recovered a related gene instead.

30
00:02:20.457 --> 00:02:23.137
It was reported in 1989 as TrkB.

31
00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:29.397
A later search, with Fabienne Lamballe, found a third relative, TrkC, reported in

32
00:02:29.397 --> 00:02:30.397
1991.

33
00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:34.587
A search for one receptor had turned up a family of three.

34
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:39.230
That is why the original Trk protein is now called TrkA.

35
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:41.850
One question stayed open.

36
00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:47.330
How p75 and TrkA together give high-affinity binding is still argued.

37
00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:53.610
The takeaway: the survival receptor was found in cancer, and NGF turned it on.
